## Naming Conventions
- Use `snake_case` for variables, functions, classes, structs, namespaces, and enum class names
- Use `UPPER_CASE` for constants, macros, and enum values
- **Note: PascalCase may appear in auto-generated code only**

### **Member Variable Naming Convention**
- **Private member variables MUST have underscore prefix (`_`)**
- **Public member variables use snake_case (no underscore prefix)**
- **Member variables MUST be declared before method declarations in class definition**

```cpp
// Preferred - Follow fb project standard
class example_class
{
private:
    std::atomic<bool> _is_running{false};        // Private: underscore prefix
    std::string       _internal_name;            // Private: underscore prefix
    mutable std::mutex _data_mutex;              // Private: underscore prefix

public:
    std::string display_name;                    // Public: no underscore prefix
    
    // Methods come after member variables
    void start();
    void stop();
    bool is_running() const { return this->_is_running.load(); }
};

// Avoid - Wrong member variable naming
class bad_example
{
public:
    void start();                                // WRONG: Methods before member variables
    
private:
    std::atomic<bool> is_running{false};         // WRONG: Missing underscore prefix
    std::string       internalName;              // WRONG: camelCase instead of snake_case
    mutable std::mutex data_mutex_;              // WRONG: Underscore suffix instead of prefix
};
```

### **Enum Naming Convention**
```cpp
// Preferred - Follow fb project standard
enum class trade::state : uint8_t
{
    REQUEST    = 0x00,
    UP_ITEM    = 0x01,
    ITEM_COUNT = 0x02,
    UP_MONEY   = 0x03,
    CANCEL     = 0x04,
    LOCK       = 0x05,
};

// Pattern:
// - enum class name: snake_case
// - enum values: UPPER_CASE with underscores
```

## Control Flow and Code Style

### **Single-Line if-else Statements**
For simple if-else statements where all blocks are single-line, omit braces:
```cpp
// Preferred
if (condition)
    single_statement();
else if (other_condition)
    another_statement();
else
    default_statement();

// Avoid
if (condition)
{
    single_statement();
}
else
{
    another_statement();
}
```

### **Loop Statements**
Always use braces for loop statements, even if single-line:
```cpp
// Preferred
for (auto i = 0; i < size; i++)
{
    process_item(i);
}

while (condition)
{
    single_operation();
}

// Avoid
for (auto i = 0; i < size; i++)
    process_item(i);

while (condition)
    single_operation();
```

### **Variable Initialization Patterns**
Prefer initializing variables with default values and updating conditionally:
```cpp
// Preferred
auto success = false;
if (index != 0xFF)
    success = ch->items.store(index, count);

// Alternative (for simple cases)
auto success = (index == 0xFF) ? false : ch->items.store(index, count);

// Avoid
bool success;
if (index == 0xFF)
{
    success = false;
}
else
{
    success = ch->items.store(index, count);
}
```

### **auto with Pointer Types**
When using `auto` with pointer initialization, use `static_cast` to specify the correct pointer type:
```cpp
// Preferred
auto returned = static_cast<fb::game::item*>(nullptr);
auto player   = static_cast<character*>(nullptr);

// Avoid (type becomes std::nullptr_t)
auto returned = nullptr;
auto player   = nullptr;

// Also acceptable when type is clear from context
auto returned = some_function_returning_item_ptr();
auto player   = find_character(id);
```

### **Benefits of This Style**
- Reduces visual clutter
- Emphasizes the main logic flow
- Prevents uninitialized variable usage
- More readable for simple conditional assignments

## Modern C++ Guidelines
- Use modern C++ features (C++17/20)
- Follow RAII principles
- Prefer `std::unique_ptr` and `std::shared_ptr` over raw pointers
- Use `const` wherever possible
- **Prefer `auto` for variable declarations when type is clear**
- **Use `this->` keyword when accessing member variables/functions for clarity**

## Example Preferred Style
```cpp
auto player = this->find_player(player_id);
auto& inventory = this->get_inventory();
```

## Include Guards
- **Use traditional `#ifndef`, `#define`, `#endif` pattern (NOT `#pragma once`)**

## Doxygen Documentation (MANDATORY)
- **MANDATORY: ALL C++ functions MUST have complete documentation**
- **ZERO TOLERANCE for missing documentation on public functions, classes, and methods**
- **Documentation MUST be 100% accurate and match function signature exactly**
- **Grammar and syntax MUST be perfect - no awkward phrasing allowed**
- **Parameter descriptions MUST match actual parameter names and types**
- **Return value descriptions MUST accurately describe what is returned**
- **Proper indentation is MANDATORY for all documentation comments**
- **Always specify parameter direction (in/out/inout)**

## Doxygen Format Example
```cpp
/**
 * @brief   Handles client connection and message processing
 * 
 *          This function processes incoming messages from connected clients
 *          and manages the game state accordingly.
 * 
 * @param[in]  client_id Unique identifier for the client
 * @param[in]  message   Incoming message to process
 * @param[out] result    Processing result status
 * @return  true if processing succeeded, false otherwise
 * 
 * @note    This function is thread-safe
 * @warning Ensure client_id is valid before calling
 */
bool process_client_message(uint32_t client_id, const message& message, process_result& result);
```

## Error Handling
- Use exceptions for exceptional cases
- Prefer error codes for expected failures
- Always handle resource cleanup (RAII)
- Use `auto` for exception handling when type is clear

## Performance Guidelines
- Profile before optimizing
- Use object pooling for frequently allocated objects
- Minimize memory allocations in hot paths
- Cache expensive computations when appropriate


